            {
              "id": "3de19b7f-8172-3c4b-9c5e-824f298bbd8e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination is a fixed-size character array, and the source is an identifier, with evidence of aliasing between them. The analysis specifically notes that an alias assignment was observed, indicating that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory. This can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. There are no explicit checks or guards to prevent this overlap, and the function used (memcpy) does not handle overlapping memory safely. The source and destination are both non-null, so null pointer dereference is not a concern here. The risk is solely due to the potential for overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the memory copy. This can be done by comparing the addresses and sizes of the buffers. If overlap is possible, handle the copy differently or avoid it.\n\n```c++\nif ((hipsparse_rev + sizeof(v) <= v) || (v + sizeof(v) <= hipsparse_rev)) {\n    memcpy(hipsparse_rev, v, sizeof(v));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or avoid the copy\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely.\n\n```c++\nmemmove(hipsparse_rev, v, sizeof(v));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
