            {
              "id": "83121edd-d17e-306b-bf4a-344972863d85",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "50b5f0dc-d814-37ae-afbc-30e8d07a2103",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be a serious security issue, especially when these values are used in security-sensitive contexts, such as generating encryption keys, session identifiers, or other secret values.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. The `rand()` function is a weak random number generator that is not suitable for cryptographic purposes. It generates pseudo-random numbers that can be easily predicted, especially if an attacker can guess or know the seed value.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong, cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937 generator). These functions provide a higher quality of randomness and are more suitable for cryptographic purposes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nhalpha = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a truly random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister generator (`std::mt19937`). The `std::uniform_real_distribution` function is used to generate a random double between 0 and 1, similar to the original code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "50b5f0dc-d814-37ae-afbc-30e8d07a2103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fdba2011-2910-3692-809e-04f49314aa12",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be a serious security issue, especially when these values are used in security-sensitive contexts, such as generating encryption keys, session identifiers, or other secret values.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. The `rand()` function is a weak random number generator that is not suitable for cryptographic purposes. It generates pseudo-random numbers that can be easily predicted, especially if an attacker can guess or know the seed value.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong, cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937 generator). These functions provide a higher quality of randomness and are more suitable for cryptographic purposes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nhalpha = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a truly random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister generator (`std::mt19937`). The `std::uniform_real_distribution` function is used to generate a random double between 0 and 1, similar to the original code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
